Subject: Unwanted extra space at top of legend
Message-ID: <V7pu2OncZDg.IVqpBHkd1ZMJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/V7pu2OncZDg/m/IVqpBHkd1ZMJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

In the example at the end of this message, the legend background
covers more space above the legend items than it does on the other
three sides.  I assume this has something to do with leaving space for
the title, but since I have removed the legend title, I want that
extra space to go away.  How do I do that?  None of the options
settings seem to be what I want.

(This is simplified from a plot with the legend placed inside the plot
area; I'd like to get it so the legend items appear to be aligned to
the upper left-hand corner of the plot, but right now if I place it
appropriately, the legend background box overlaps the strip area.)

(Wouldn't it be nice if it were possible to place the legend relative
to a particular panel's plot area rather than to the overall page?)

zw

library(grid)
library(ggplot2)
library(reshape2)

dat <- melt(data.frame(x=1:20,
                       a=cumsum(rnorm(20)),
                       b=cumsum(runif(20)),
                       c=cumsum(rexp(20))),
            id.vars='x')

ggplot(dat, aes(x=x, y=value, colour=variable)) +
  geom_line() +
  opts(legend.title=theme_blank(),
       legend.key.height=unit(0.6,"lines"),
       legend.background=theme_rect(colour="black"),
       legend.margin=unit(0,"cm"))

From u5ec5102a43e29af7 at groups.google.com  Fri Aug  3 12:57:01 2012
From: u5ec5102a43e29af7 at groups.google.com (smu)
Date: Fri, 03 Aug 2012 12:57:01 +0000
Subject: scale_x_date, date_breaks and start date
Message-ID: <IVCpSNSszv0.028K0Hx8VPIJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/IVCpSNSszv0/m/028K0Hx8VPIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear all,

consider the following example:

library(ggplot2)
library(scales)
df <- data.frame(date = seq.Date(as.Date('1903-01-01'),
                         by = 'years', length.out = 100),
        y = rnorm(100) )

p <- ggplot(df, aes(date, y)) + geom_line()
print(p)

p + scale_x_date('', breaks = date_breaks("10 years"),
             labels = date_format("%Y"))

The first ggplot call produces a nicely scaled x-axis in 20 year steps,
starting with 1900. However, I want 10 year steps, so I tried the
breaks argument. Now the axis starts in 1908 (in general first year in df +
0.5 * date_breaks), which is not what I want.

Is there a way to influence the x-axis start date in combination with
date_breaks?

thanks!

cheers,
 stefan

From u373beb702917b8b5 at groups.google.com  Fri Aug  3 13:58:28 2012
From: u373beb702917b8b5 at groups.google.com (Ista Zahn)
Date: Fri, 03 Aug 2012 13:58:28 +0000
Subject: Re: scale_x_date, date_breaks and start date
Message-ID: <IVCpSNSszv0.QnHCh0_6jIsJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <IVCpSNSszv0.028K0Hx8VPIJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <IVCpSNSszv0.028K0Hx8VPIJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/IVCpSNSszv0/m/QnHCh0_6jIsJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

HI Stefan,

You can use the expand argument for this. After much trial and errror
I find that

p + scale_x_date('', breaks = date_breaks("10 years"),

                 labels = date_format("%Y"),
                 expand=c(.13, 1))

gives the desired result.

Best,
Ista

