>>
>> This is an interesting way to add CIs to a plot
>>
>> The idea is that broad CI-shading (like ggplot currently gives) attracts
>> the eye to the _least_ reliable ends of the data.
>>
>> The alternative is to instead set transparency = CI width.
>>
>> That draws the eye to the section with solid data, and hints with a
>> washed-out shade at the regions we're less sure of.
>>
>> It's described here:
>>
>>
>> http://andrewgelman.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/08/HSIANG_VISUALLY_WEIGHTED_REGRESSION_v1.pdf
>>
>>
>> I still find Figure 1 the most informative of all: just a line and the
>> data points :-)
>>
>> t
>>
>
> There is some interesting discussion of this at Andrew Gelman's blog
> http://andrewgelman.com/2012/08/visually-weighting-regression-displays/ .
> I like the shading or blending idea, if I recall the term correctly but I
> don't think I see exactly how to do it in ggplot2.

I recall a discussion about adjusting the transparency of a (fixed
width) bar where the transparency is based on the uncertainty. I can't
find that discussion now, unfortunately. One approach that came out of
that used geom_rect to draw individual parts based on what was extracted
from stat_smooth, if I recall correctly.

