From u5a37f1244321fb07 at groups.google.com  Tue Aug 21 13:30:51 2012
From: u5a37f1244321fb07 at groups.google.com (Ito, Kaori [Groton])
Date: Tue, 21 Aug 2012 13:30:51 +0000
Subject: RE: stat_sum uses numbers with decimals, but data only has rounded numbers
Message-ID: <0YO0Ez3Z10s.Fy09AOkxoBEJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <0YO0Ez3Z10s.eHSD6uF_dwIJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <0YO0Ez3Z10s.eHSD6uF_dwIJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/0YO0Ez3Z10s/m/Fy09AOkxoBEJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Alexander,
I don't know the answer for the "stat_sum" issue, but I think I know why "scale_x_continuous" behaves like this.

In your original code:

...
xlim(0, 215) +

scale_x_continuous("Inhabitants in thousands") +

...

The default setting for limits is "NULL" for scale_x_continuous, so x-limits are overwritten (to NULL) even you specify it before.

Therefore, if you switch the order as:

scale_x_continuous("Inhabitants in thousands") +

xlim(0, 215) +
...

...then, it will work.

Or, you can specify the limits within scale_x_continuous:

scale_x_continuous("Inhabitants in thousands", limits=c(0,215)) +

Hope this helps...
Kaori

