Kaori, I had come to the same conclusion in my first reply to this thread, but thank you for your willingness help nevertheless.

I'm a bit disappointed that the developers of ggplot2 who know about the stat_sum functionality apparently didn't read this message. Because this is obviously a bug I will I post this on the bug tracker – https://github.com/hadley/ggplot2/issues – for ggplot2. Strange that the bug tracker is not advertised on the ggplot2 website.

On Tuesday, August 21, 2012 3:30:51 PM UTC+2, Kaori Ito wrote:
> Hi Alexander,
> I don't know the answer for the "stat_sum" issue, but I think I know why "scale_x_continuous" behaves like this.
>
>
> In your original code:
>
> ...
> xlim(0, 215) +
> scale_x_continuous("Inhabitants in thousands") +
> ...
>
> The default setting for limits is "NULL" for scale_x_continuous, so x-limits are overwritten (to NULL) even you specify it before.
>
> Therefore, if you switch the order as:
>
> scale_x_continuous("Inhabitants in thousands") +
> xlim(0, 215) +
> ...
>
> ...then, it will work.
>
> Or, you can specify the limits within scale_x_continuous:
>
> scale_x_continuous("Inhabitants in thousands", limits=c(0,215)) +
>
> Hope this helps...
> Kaori
>
>
> -----Original Message-----
> From: ggp...@googlegroups.com [mailto:ggp...@googlegroups.com] On Behalf Of Winston Chang
> Sent: Monday, August 20, 2012 5:20 PM
> To: Alexander van Loon
> Cc: ggp...@googlegroups.com
> Subject: Re: stat_sum uses numbers with decimals, but data only has rounded numbers
>
> Yes, this behavior can be a bit confusing. In the upcoming version of
> ggplot2, it also provides a warning message if you have xlim with
> scale_x_continuous.
>
> -Winston
>
